 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Correlation Term:** The 2D integral with the correlation term $b(x-\hat{\mu}_x)(y-\hat{\mu}_y)$ is intractable analytically [S1]. The method resolves this by diagonalizing the covariance matrix and rotating the integration domain to align with eigenvectors, approximating the 2D integral as a product of two independent 1D integrals [S1].
-*   **Small Standard Deviation:** Unlike Mip-Splatting which convolves with a Gaussian kernel and suppresses high-frequency components causing over-smoothing, Analytic-Splatting calculates the integral analytically to preserve details [S1].
-*   **Super Sampling:** Unlike Monte Carlo super sampling, this method uses a closed-form approximation, avoiding the computation burden of increasing sample counts [S1].
+*   **Correlation Term:** The 2D Gaussian integral contains a correlation term $b(x-\hat{\mu}_x)(y-\hat{\mu}_y)$ which is intractable analytically in the original coordinate system. The method resolves this by diagonalizing the covariance matrix and rotating the integration domain to align with eigenvectors, approximating the 2D integral as a product of two independent 1D integrals [S1].
+*   **Small Standard Deviation:** Unlike Mip-Splatting, which convolves the Gaussian signal with a Gaussian kernel and may introduce large gaps for high-frequency components (small $\sigma$), Analytic-Splatting calculates the integral analytically, avoiding this issue [S1].
+*   **Precision:** The logistic approximation coefficients (1.6 and 0.07) are fixed constants [S1].
